nommu-bank: swap the resident pages instead of keeping one backup per process

The backend gave every process its own page set, including the one whose
memory is resident. That set is dead weight: the resident process's data is in
the region itself, and its backup is only written when it stops running. So a
region shared by N processes cost N page sets when N-1 are enough, and the
first fork of a region allocated two of them -- the parent's and the child's --
for a process whose memory had not diverged at all yet.

Exchange the resident page with the incoming shadow page instead of saving and
then restoring. After the exchange the region holds the incoming process's data
and the shadow page holds the outgoing one's, so the page set simply changes
owner; the resident owner ends up holding none. The traffic is identical: two
loads and two stores per word is what the save-then-restore pair of memcpy()
already cost. What changes is the footprint.

  program      private  backups before  backups after
  bash            276K            552K           276K
  micropython     308K            616K           308K
  make            216K            432K           216K
  dash            140K            280K           140K

bank_detach() gains the matching case: when the process leaving was the
resident one, its copy in the region is dead, so a sibling is promoted by
restoring its saved state and handing its pages back, which is the same
invariant a switch maintains.

bank_access() keeps interrupts disabled for one page rather than for the whole
request. Its length comes from __access_remote_vm() bounded only by the size of
the VMA, so reading /proc/PID/mem of a banked process could ask for a single
512 KiB memcpy with interrupts off.

The private-memory ceiling becomes a module parameter. It is also the bound on
how long a switch runs with interrupts disabled, which is the number worth
tuning against a latency budget rather than fixing at build time.

